>"playwright" is default fixture, so you need not define it.

>headless=False -> headed mode....you can see what is going on the browser

>"page" fixture used chromium engine in headless mode by default. But when using this you can not do some customisations like switching between 2 contexts.

>CSS Selector: #id or .classname or tagname(not preferred since there are many tagnames in page)

>Keep imports absolute (never rely on relative .. imports in test suites).

>Run pytest from the project root or parent folder.

>Use pytest.ini or pyproject.toml to enforce consistent paths across local dev, PyCharm, and CI/CD.

>Keep __init__.py files empty unless you want to expose shortcuts.

>Put shared fixtures in conftest.py at the package root → pytest auto‑discovers them.

>Use session scope for static data (like credentials, configs).

>Keep fixtures modular: one fixture per responsibility (credentials, browser, API client, etc.).

>Avoid hard‑coding paths → always resolve relative to __file__.

>for multiproject setup:
    -One pytest.ini per project → isolates test discovery.
    -Run "pytest" command from project root or specify the project folder explicitly if running from parent folder(PycharmProjects).
    e.g. pytest PythonPlaywright
    -Use conftest.py per project for fixtures local to that project.
    -CI/CD pipelines run pytest per project, not globally.

>ide level run mode changes wont be considered if you are running tests from command line

>"-m" stands for marker expression
add markers such as smoke, regression and register them in pytest.ini
to run particular marker tests use below cmd command
pytest -m smoke
You can combine marker expressions:
pytest -m "smoke or regression" → runs both smoke and regression tests.
pytest -m "smoke and not regression" → runs tests marked as smoke but excludes regression.

>"-k" is a keyword expression filter.
to run particular tests with particular keyword in function names, class names, or test file names)
Below command also executed test_javascript_injection_of_sessionCookies.py may be because it is in Network_interception folder
pytest -k network
more examples:
pytest -k "abc or xyz" → runs tests with "abc" or "xyz" in their names.
pytest -k "abc and not xyz" → runs tests with "abc" but excludes those with "xyz".

>"-n" no. of workers
running tests in parallel
needs external package pytest-xdist to be installed.
Bellow command will run 3 tests in parallel
pytest -n 3
Parallel execution works best when tests are independent/atomic (no shared state, no reliance on order).
pytest -n auto → automatically uses as many workers as CPU cores.

>"--html" -> to generate html report
needs external package pytest-html to be installed.
below command will generate report.html in reports folder. The additional CSS/JS is saved in assets folder.
pytest --html=reports/report.html
Extra options:
--self-contained-html → embeds CSS/JS directly into the report so it’s portable (no external assets).
--html=report.html --css=style.css → lets you apply custom styling.
e.g. pytest --html=reports/report.html --self-contained-html
pytest --html=reports/report.html --self-contained-html -k network
below is pytest.ini setup that automatically generates these HTML reports every time you run tests, so you don’t have to type the flag manually
# pytest.ini
[pytest]
addopts = --html=reports/report.html --self-contained-html
Here addopts → adds default command‑line options for every pytest run so you dont have to specify them separately each time while running tests.

